{
  "id": "imperativ",
  "name": "Imperative: habla, no hables, dímelo",
  "level": "B1",
  "variety": "es-ES",
  "es419": { "note": "Latin America has no vosotros forms (hablad, sentaos): the plural is always ustedes (hablen, siéntense). Some regions use vos with its own forms." },
  "subtitle": "Spanish has four forms: tú (habla), usted (hable), vosotros (hablad), ustedes (hablen). Negative forms use the subjunctive: no hables, no hable, no habléis, no hablen. Pronouns go on the end of an affirmative form and before a negative one: dímelo, no me lo digas.",
  "briefMarks": ["habla", "hable", "hablad", "hablen", "no hables", "no habléis", "dímelo", "no me lo digas"],
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-11-04)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "tú: habla · no hables", "variant": "verb" },
      { "text": "usted: hable · no hable", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "vosotros: hablad · no habléis", "variant": "verb" },
      { "text": "ustedes: hablen · no hablen", "variant": "prep" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-verb\">Abre</span> la ventana. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Espere</span> aquí, por favor. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">No llegues</span> tarde. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Dímelo</span>. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">No me lo digas</span>. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Siéntese</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>tú, affirmative:</strong> the same as the <em>él</em> form of the present: <em>habla, come, escribe, cierra, vuelve, pide</em>. Eight common verbs are short and irregular: <em>di, haz, ve, pon, sal, sé, ten, ven</em>.",
        "tag": "tú"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>usted and ustedes:</strong> swap the vowel. <em>-ar</em> verbs take <em>e</em>: <em>hable, hablen</em>. <em>-er, -ir</em> verbs take <em>a</em>: <em>coma, escriban</em>. Irregulars start from the <em>yo</em> form: <em>tengo → tenga, hago → haga, digo → diga, vengo → venga</em>. Also <em>vaya, sea, dé, esté</em>.",
        "tag": "usted, ustedes"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>vosotros, affirmative:</strong> change the <em>r</em> of the infinitive to <em>d</em>: <em>hablar → hablad, comer → comed, venir → venid</em>. No verb is irregular here. In speech, many people say the infinitive (<em>¡Venir!</em>), but <em>venid</em> is the correct form.",
        "tag": "vosotros"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>All negatives use the subjunctive</strong>: <em>no hables, no hable, no habléis, no hablen; no comas, no coma, no comáis, no coman</em>. So <em>no vengas</em>, never ✗ <em>no ven</em>, and <em>no hagas</em>, never ✗ <em>no haz</em>.",
        "tag": "Negative"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Pronouns:</strong> on the end of an affirmative form, before a negative one. <em>Dímelo. No me lo digas. Llámame. No me llames.</em> The stress stays, so you often need an accent: <em>levántate, siéntese, dámelo</em>.",
        "tag": "With pronouns"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Reflexive vosotros</strong> loses the <em>d</em>: <em>levantaos, sentaos, callaos</em>. The verb <em>irse</em> is <em>idos</em>, though <em>iros</em> is very common in speech.",
        "tag": "levantaos"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>No habla</em> as a command → ✓ <em>No hables</em>. ✗ <em>No me dígalo</em> → ✓ <em>No me lo diga</em>. ✗ <em>Levantate</em> → ✓ <em>Levántate</em>. ✗ <em>Sentaros</em> → ✓ <em>Sentaos</em>. Be polite with people you don't know: use <em>usted</em> and add <em>por favor</em>.",
    "reference": {
